or aspect of the specimen and \u000aexits through the posterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is received \u000awith a radiograph which demonstrates a wire and numerous calcifications within \u000athe specimen. The specimen has been previously inked per the surgeon per \u000apathology protocol (superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral \u000ayellow, anterior blue, posterior black). The specimen is serially sectioned \u000afrom superior to inferior. Upon sectioning the specimen is markedly friable \u000awith admixed suture material throughout the entire specimen. There are focal \u000aareas of irregular dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately 60% of \u000athe breast parenchyma. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is tan/yellow \u000aand homogeneous with no discrete lesions grossly identified. The specimen is \u000asubmitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Superior margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Sequential cross sections superior to inferior, block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to \u000ainclude area of possible calcifications, now designated by needle localization \u000awire, blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to include adjacent breast parenchyma designated per needle \u000alocalization wire, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Inferior margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000asek/elw \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a11/14/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
